WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:06.162
Take two to the n, minus one

2
00:00:06.162 --> 00:00:08.975
for n from two to seven.

3
00:00:08.975 --> 00:00:12.912
Three, seven, fifteen, thirty-one

4
00:00:12.912 --> 00:00:17.300
sixty-three, one hundred twenty-seven.

5
00:00:17.650 --> 00:00:20.963
Three is prime, seven is prime

6
00:00:20.963 --> 00:00:23.207
thirty-one is prime.

7
00:00:23.207 --> 00:00:27.374
A claim suggests itself: two to the n

8
00:00:27.374 --> 00:00:28.550
minus one

9
00:00:28.550 --> 00:00:32.290
is prime for every n at least two.

10
00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:33.360
Is it?

11
00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:35.520
Pause and decide.

12
00:00:39.100 --> 00:00:39.391
No.

13
00:00:39.391 --> 00:00:41.624
Look at n equals four.

14
00:00:41.624 --> 00:00:44.439
Fifteen is three times five

15
00:00:44.439 --> 00:00:46.380
so it is not prime.

16
00:00:46.730 --> 00:00:49.051
The claim was about every n

17
00:00:49.051 --> 00:00:52.117
so one failure is enough to sink it.

18
00:00:52.117 --> 00:00:54.521
Fifteen is a counterexample.

19
00:00:54.521 --> 00:00:58.250
Sixty-three fails too, but we only need one.

20
00:00:58.600 --> 00:01:00.712
Now change two words.

21
00:01:00.712 --> 00:01:04.535
There is an n for which two to the n

22
00:01:04.535 --> 00:01:07.049
minus one, is not prime.

23
00:01:07.049 --> 00:01:09.363
That sentence is true

24
00:01:09.363 --> 00:01:12.280
and n equals four proves it.

25
00:01:12.860 --> 00:01:17.146
The two sentences differ in two small words.

26
00:01:17.146 --> 00:01:20.847
For all, written as an upside-down A

27
00:01:20.847 --> 00:01:24.159
means for every element of a set.

28
00:01:24.159 --> 00:01:26.983
There exists, a backwards E

29
00:01:26.983 --> 00:01:30.100
means for at least one element.

30
00:01:30.450 --> 00:01:32.876
The set is called the domain

31
00:01:32.876 --> 00:01:35.638
and it is part of the statement.

32
00:01:35.721 --> 00:01:38.148
Here the domain is the values

33
00:01:38.231 --> 00:01:39.570
of n we look at.

34
00:01:39.920 --> 00:01:44.309
On a finite domain, for all is a long and

35
00:01:44.309 --> 00:01:46.608
every cell must pass.

36
00:01:46.608 --> 00:01:49.430
There exists is a long or

37
00:01:49.430 --> 00:01:51.520
one cell is enough.

38
00:01:52.100 --> 00:01:55.911
How do you prove a there-exists statement?

39
00:01:55.911 --> 00:01:59.904
Claim: there are positive integers a and b

40
00:01:59.904 --> 00:02:00.902
not equal

41
00:02:00.902 --> 00:02:04.260
with a to the b equal to b to the a.

42
00:02:04.610 --> 00:02:07.494
Here is a table of a to the b

43
00:02:07.494 --> 00:02:10.378
for a and b from two to four.

44
00:02:10.378 --> 00:02:13.262
Two to the fourth is sixteen.

45
00:02:13.262 --> 00:02:16.530
And four squared is also sixteen.

46
00:02:16.880 --> 00:02:18.717
That is the whole proof.

47
00:02:18.717 --> 00:02:19.942
Name a witness

48
00:02:19.942 --> 00:02:22.391
a equals two and b equals four

49
00:02:22.391 --> 00:02:25.300
and check it: sixteen equals sixteen.

50
00:02:25.376 --> 00:02:27.520
How you found the witness is

51
00:02:27.596 --> 00:02:29.280
not part of the proof.

52
00:02:29.860 --> 00:02:32.974
A for-all statement needs more.

53
00:02:32.974 --> 00:02:35.786
Claim: for every integer n

54
00:02:35.786 --> 00:02:38.900
three divides n cubed minus n.

55
00:02:39.250 --> 00:02:42.619
Try n equals five: one hundred and twenty

56
00:02:42.619 --> 00:02:44.865
which is three times forty.

57
00:02:44.865 --> 00:02:48.474
But that proves only the case n equals five.

58
00:02:48.474 --> 00:02:51.362
There are infinitely many integers

59
00:02:51.362 --> 00:02:54.410
and examples cover only some of them.

60
00:02:54.990 --> 00:02:59.066
Instead, let n be an arbitrary integer

61
00:02:59.066 --> 00:03:02.620
and assume nothing else about it.

62
00:03:02.620 --> 00:03:06.801
Factor: n cubed minus n is n minus one

63
00:03:06.801 --> 00:03:09.623
times n, times n plus one.

64
00:03:09.623 --> 00:03:12.550
Three consecutive integers.

65
00:03:12.900 --> 00:03:14.548
On the number line

66
00:03:14.635 --> 00:03:16.890
the multiples of three are

67
00:03:16.977 --> 00:03:18.538
every third point.

68
00:03:18.625 --> 00:03:21.313
Three consecutive integers make

69
00:03:21.400 --> 00:03:23.482
a window of width three.

70
00:03:23.482 --> 00:03:25.823
Slide the window anywhere

71
00:03:25.910 --> 00:03:27.905
it always holds exactly

72
00:03:27.992 --> 00:03:29.900
one multiple of three.

73
00:03:30.250 --> 00:03:32.504
So one of the three factors

74
00:03:32.587 --> 00:03:34.507
is a multiple of three

75
00:03:34.507 --> 00:03:36.593
and so is their product.

76
00:03:36.593 --> 00:03:38.513
Since n was arbitrary

77
00:03:38.597 --> 00:03:40.850
three divides n cubed minus

78
00:03:40.934 --> 00:03:42.770
n for every integer n.

79
00:03:43.350 --> 00:03:46.093
Back to two to the n, minus one.

80
00:03:46.093 --> 00:03:49.607
The claim, every one is prime, is false.

81
00:03:49.607 --> 00:03:52.950
So what exactly does its negation say?

82
00:03:53.300 --> 00:03:56.262
Not every one is prime means

83
00:03:56.262 --> 00:03:58.612
some one is not prime.

84
00:03:58.612 --> 00:04:02.902
Move the not inside, past the quantifier

85
00:04:02.902 --> 00:04:06.580
and flip for all into there exists.

86
00:04:07.160 --> 00:04:09.822
A common mistake is to write

87
00:04:09.822 --> 00:04:12.024
every one is not prime.

88
00:04:12.024 --> 00:04:15.879
That sentence is false too: three, seven

89
00:04:15.971 --> 00:04:18.357
thirty-one and one hundred

90
00:04:18.449 --> 00:04:20.560
twenty-seven are prime.

91
00:04:20.910 --> 00:04:23.658
A statement and its negation always

92
00:04:23.736 --> 00:04:25.856
have opposite truth values.

93
00:04:25.856 --> 00:04:29.310
If both are false, you have not negated it.

94
00:04:29.660 --> 00:04:33.155
The other rule flips the other way

95
00:04:33.155 --> 00:04:37.050
not there exists becomes for all, not.

96
00:04:37.050 --> 00:04:40.844
To disprove a there-exists statement

97
00:04:40.844 --> 00:04:43.940
show that every element fails.

98
00:04:44.520 --> 00:04:47.068
So: to prove there exists

99
00:04:47.068 --> 00:04:49.910
name a witness and check it.

100
00:04:49.910 --> 00:04:54.320
To prove for all, take an arbitrary element.

101
00:04:54.670 --> 00:04:56.566
To disprove for all

102
00:04:56.566 --> 00:04:59.409
one counterexample is enough.

103
00:04:59.409 --> 00:05:03.105
And to negate, move the not inside and

104
00:05:03.200 --> 00:05:05.190
flip each quantifier.

105
00:05:05.770 --> 00:05:10.525
Try it yourself: is two to the p, minus one

106
00:05:10.525 --> 00:05:13.227
prime for every prime p?

107
00:05:13.227 --> 00:05:17.010
Find a proof, or a counterexample.

108
00:05:17.360 --> 00:05:18.497
In part two

109
00:05:18.497 --> 00:05:21.624
two quantifiers in one sentence.

110
00:05:21.624 --> 00:05:24.561
For every lock there is a key

111
00:05:24.561 --> 00:05:27.783
or there is a key for every lock

112
00:05:27.783 --> 00:05:30.720
the order changes the meaning.
